{"agent":"fleet-failedgate","data":{"by":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash","evidence":{"chunk_findings":[{"chunk":1,"detail":"The message still presents `git worktree remove {wt.path}` as the actionable step, which is precisely the command the bug says fails on a dirty tree. Given the repro in the intent — a worktree at the item's path with another branch checked out and a modified file — the newly worded advice reads \"If it is yours to discard, `git worktree remove <path>`, then `ddflow claim <item>` again\"; running that exact sequence fails with `fatal: '<path>' contains modified or untracked files, use --force to delete it`. Nothing in the string adds `--force`, a clean/stash step, or any other executable remedy, and the only fallback offered (\"otherwise claim from a tree of your own\") is a sentence, not a command. So for the very input the fix targets, the refusal still contains no next command the user can actually run; the \"inspect it first\" line only tells them why it will fail, not how to proceed. The intent states the goal is to stop offering a command that fails on a dirty tree and to \"keep one exact next command per case\", but the dirty case here still resolves to the same failing `git worktree remove`.","digest":"652a17f0598ad3ab","location":"ddflow/api/lifecycle/claim.py:461-466 (occupancy refusal message)","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/api/lifecycle/claim.py:461-466 (occupancy refusal message)"}],"chunks_total":1,"coverage":"1/1 chunk(s) reviewed","delta_rounds":0,"diff_chars":1671,"diff_sha":"cc5f0674a795b21fd644ed4a27a0577704a603ef765e359dff64b678f44ddbe1","diff_source":"main..HEAD + tracked working-tree changes in [REDACTED:path]","diff_stat":{"deletions":0,"files":0,"insertions":0,"untracked":0},"elapsed_s":41.0,"family":"deepseek","findings":1,"max_chunk_chars":5000,"model":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash","output_digest":"58859c88d8c640bd","output_file":".ddflow/local/reviews/fix-Bbba290fd86.critic.lan-deepseek-v4.1-flash.20261017T