{"agent":"fleet-failedgate","data":{"by":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash","evidence":{"chunk_findings":[{"chunk":3,"detail":"The whole guard is fail-open on missing gate definitions. `defs` is typed `Mapping[str, GateDef] | None`, and the code explicitly normalizes it with `(defs or {}).get(g)`; for every pipeline gate that has no entry in that mapping the loop hits `if g in excused or gd is None or gd.reviewer != \"different_family\": continue`, so `unresolved_review_failure` is never called and `rows` stays empty. Concretely, calling `failed_review_blockers(state, cfg, item_id, None)` for the exact item in the bug report — pipeline containing `reviewer = \"different_family\"` gates `critic` and `rubber_duck`, both with latest outcome `failed` and 3 confirmed findings, 0 delta rounds — returns `[]` instead of the `FAILED_REVIEW_BLOCKER` string, so `complete`/`merge` accept the item, which is precisely the behavior this module exists to prevent. The same happens whenever `defs` is only a partial mapping (e.g. project overrides that do not repeat the built-in `critic`/`rubber_duck` definitions): a failed review gate whose definition the caller did not supply is silently treated as if it were not a review gate, rather than being blocked or reported as unverifiable. To be safe the function should treat \"pipeline says this is a gate but no def is available\" as a blocker (or refuse to run) instead of `continue`.","digest":"aa4ab9f9fb70f32b","location":"ddflow/services/gates/failed_review.py :: `failed_review_blockers` (the `gd = (defs or {}).get(g)` / `gd is None` skip)","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/services/gates/failed_review.py :: `failed_review_blockers` (the `gd = (defs or {}).get(g)` / `gd is None` skip)"}],"chunks_total":4,"coverage":"4/4 chunk(s) reviewed","delta_rounds":0,"diff_chars":19726,"diff_sha":"6dfe47df5263f97b21b3bc2be1fbf95ff46d59040fa0926feceef225ceda8656","diff_source":"main..HEAD + tracked working-tree changes in [REDACTED:path]","diff_sta